al point. The beta function would then have a zero at this non-trivial fixed point. However, perturbatively, the beta function might not show this because perturbation theory around the Gaussian fixed point is not valid for strong coupling. \n\nSo putting this together: \n\n- Near the Gaussian fixed point (\u03bb=0), the beta function is dominated by the classical term \u03b2(\u03bb)=2\u03bb, indicating that \u03bb is relevant and grows as we flow into the IR. Perturbatively, there may be small quantum corrections, but in 2D, since the theory is super-renormalizable, these corrections might be absent or calculable. \n\n- However, non-perturbatively, there is a Wilson-Fisher fixed point at some finite \u03bb where \u03b2(\u03bb)=0. This would be the critical point of the Ising model in 2D. At this point, the theory is conformally invariant. \n\nTherefore, the beta function in 2D scalar \u03c6\u2074 theory starts as \u03b2(\u03bb)=2\u03bb near the Gaussian fixed point (weak coupling), but at stronger coupling, it curves over and develops a zero at the Wilson-Fisher fixed point. This behavior is non-perturbative and can't be seen in perturbation theory around \u03bb=0. \n\nTo confirm this, consider the exact solution of the 2D Ising model. The critical point occurs at a specific temperature (related to the coupling), and the RG flow around this point has a relevant direction. The beta function would thus have a zero at this critical coupling, with the coupling flowing to the fixed point in the IR. \n\nIn summary: \n\n- As the coupling approaches zero (near the Gaussian fixed point), the beta function is \u03b2(\u03bb) \u2248 2\u03bb, indicating a relevant operator. \n\n- Near the Wilson-Fisher fixed point (non-perturbative regime), the beta function has a zero, so \u03b2(\u03bb) \u2248 -k(\u03bb - \u03bb_*), where \u03bb_* is the fixed point coupling. The negative sign indicates that the coupling is irrelevant around this fixed point. \n\nThus, the beta function varies