n one-loop, the beta function is \u03b2(\u03bb) = \u03bb [ - \u03b5 + 0 - \u03b3_\u03bb ].\n\nBut \u03b3_\u03bb is d ln Z_\u03bb/d ln \u03bc. But Z_\u03bb = 1 + \u03b4\u03bb/\u03bb. At one-loop, \u03b4\u03bb = (3 \u03bb)/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5), so Z_\u03bb = 1 + (3 \u03bb)/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5). Then, ln Z_\u03bb \u2248 (3 \u03bb)/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5). Therefore, \u03b3_\u03bb = d ln Z_\u03bb/d ln \u03bc = (3/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5)) \u03b2(\u03bb). But this leads to a term involving \u03b2(\u03bb) on both sides. However, to leading order, we can approximate \u03b3_\u03bb as (3/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5)) \u03b2(\u03bb). But \u03b2(\u03bb) is of order \u03bb^2, so \u03b3_\u03bb is of order \u03bb^2 / \u03b5. But in the expression for \u03b2(\u03bb):\n\n\u03b2(\u03bb) = \u03bb [ - \u03b5 - \u03b3_\u03bb ] \u2248 \u03bb [ - \u03b5 - (3/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5)) \u03b2(\u03bb) ].\n\nBut solving for \u03b2(\u03bb):\n\n\u03b2(\u03bb) + (3 \u03bb/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5)) \u03b2(\u03bb) = - \u03b5 \u03bb.\n\nTherefore, \u03b2(\u03bb) [1 + (3 \u03bb)/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5)] = - \u03b5 \u03bb.\n\nAssuming that (3 \u03bb)/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5) is small (which it isn't, since \u03b5 is small), but in the limit \u03b5 \u2192 0, this term dominates, so:\n\n\u03b2(\u03bb) \u2248 - \u03b5 \u03bb / (3 \u03bb/(16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5)) ) = - (16 \u03c0^2 \u03b5^2)/(3) ).\n\nThis is going to zero, which is not helpful. Clearly, this approach is not correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the MS scheme, we can directly compute the beta function from the counterterms without worrying about the field renormalization since Z_phi is 1 at one-loop. So in that case, the previous calculation where \u03b2(\u03bb) = -3 \u03bb^2/(16 \u03c0^2) is correct, but contradicts standard references. Therefore, there must be a sign mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, maybe the issue is with the definition of \u03b5. If we define d = 4 - 2\u03b5, then the pole is 1/\u03b5. In many textbooks, dimensional regularization is done by setting d = 4 